DECIDED, BUILD IT -- Zach, human-directed, 2026-07-28, relayed by scheduler. This is not another observation; it is the go-ahead to WIRE 'name what it retires' as a check. It supersedes the 'suggested/worth considering' framing of the three .idea drops filed earlier tonight (FAILURE-PATTERN-ecosystem-wide-20260728-001441, FAILURE-MODE-filed-by-schedule-20260728-001456, and the NAMING drop) -- read those for the evidence, treat this as the decision. THE RULE TODAY: BUILD-DISCIPLINE.md carries 'New mechanism names what it retires?' as a checklist line. It is prose, verified by a human remembering to verify it, and hygiene-lint has no rule for it. It failed on 2026-07-28 inside the very session that named the failure: scheduler built 'scheduler ask' to retire a hand-typed QUESTIONS.md format, committed it, reported it done, and left four documents still teaching the retired format. Zach caught it by reading, no check did. THE KEY INSIGHT TO BUILD AGAINST: a commit message SAYING what it retires satisfies the prose rule while the retired thing is still live. Satisfaction must mean the retired thing is GONE, and that is mechanically decidable in a way 'did you remember' never is. SUGGESTED SHAPE, yours to improve: (a) a commit/change that claims a retirement names the retired artifact in a machine-readable way -- a 'Retires:' trailer in the commit message, or a 'RETIRES <literal>' line in the new mechanism's own header, which scheduler's bin/questions-lint.sh already does informally; (b) hygiene-lint greps the repo (and, for ecosystem-wide conventions, every registered project) for that literal shape; (c) any surviving occurrence OUTSIDE a retirement notice is a FLAG: 'retirement incomplete -- <n> live occurrences of the format <x> claims to retire'. (d) the inverse check is worth as much: a doc paragraph asserting a rule for which no executable check exists is itself an unretired layer waiting to happen -- that is the general form of what bit scheduler on 2026-07-27 when a prose convention was the entire fix and lasted one day. TWO REQUIREMENTS this ecosystem has already paid for, both non-negotiable: it must exit with a distinct BLIND status when it parses zero retirement claims (a checker that silently finds nothing reads as 'all retirements complete'), and it must be WIRED to a real cadence in the same change that builds it -- scheduler's bin/blockers-freshness-check.sh sat built-but-unwired for two days, and the questions-lint built tonight was wired into 'scheduler sweep' in its own commit precisely to not repeat that.
